Template queries for the sparql-professors-universities family.
All templates were executed with rdflib 6.1 against the v01 anchor graph and
verified row sets. ADAPT PREFIXES/PREDICATES TO THE ACTUAL GRAPH before use
(run `python3 scripts/check_query.py <graph> <query>` after writing).

The anchor's correct result has 7 rows of (?professorName ?universityName)
and none of: Jane Petterson (Lecturer), the three Students, and no second
NTUA row for Felix Wagner.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
T1. Canonical: worksAt chain, depth-robust (use when persons have a preferred
employment link to a department OR directly to the university).
    ?org worksAt-from-person, then any-length partOfUniversity* path, then the
    node filtered by type uni:University. This is the default template.

PREFIX uni: <http://university.org/ontology#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?professorName ?universityName WHERE {
  ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
             uni:role ?role ;
             uni:worksAt ?org .
  ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
  ?university a uni:University ;
              uni:hasName ?universityName .
  FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
}

Notes:
  - partOfUniversity* with the type filter covers both direct affiliation
    (zero-length step) and department->faculty->university chains.
  - Do NOT add `a uni:Person` to the professor pattern: person typing may be
    absent or renamed in variants.
  - If hops use different predicates, switch the path to an alternation:
    ?org (uni:partOfUniversity|uni:subOrganizationOf)* ?university .
  - regex "professor" case-insensitive includes "Professor of ..." and
    "Assistant/Associate Professor ..." but not "Lecturer"/"Student".
  - If the instruction enumerates an exact rank set, replace the regex with
    a disjunction, e.g. regex(?role, "^(Full|Assistant) Professor", "i").
  - If role strings abbreviate the rank (Prof, Prof., Full Prof of X), use the
    abbreviation-aware form, verified to also drop "professional staff" and
    "Professor's Assistant":
    regex(?role, "(^|\\s)(prof\\.?|professor)(\\s|$)", "i")
    (It still keeps "Assistant to the Professor" — post-check passed VALUES
    and block leftovers with an explicit disjunction.)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
T2. Same rank filter but rank stored as RESOURCES with a label (spec axis 1
variant: uni:hasAcademicRank + uni:rankLabel):

PREFIX uni: <http://university.org/ontology#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?professorName ?universityName WHERE {
  ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
             uni:hasAcademicRank ?rank ;
             uni:worksAt ?org .
  ?rank uni:rankLabel ?role .
  ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
  ?university a uni:University ;
              uni:hasName ?universityName .
  FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
}

If ranks are individually typed (e.g. ?rank a uni:FullProfessor), filter with
  ?rank a ?rankClass . FILTER (regex(str(?rankClass), "Professor"))  -- or an
or-union of the observed rank classes.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
T3. Preferred worksAt + teaching fallback (spec axis 2 variant: some
qualifying professors lack the preferred relation; use their courses).
The NOT EXISTS guard is MANDATORY so professors who DO have worksAt never
leak through the teaching branch (Felix trap: anchor has 8 rows without the
guard, 7 with it).

PREFIX uni: <http://university.org/ontology#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?professorName ?universityName WHERE {
  {
    ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
               uni:role ?role ;
               uni:worksAt ?org .
    ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
    ?university a uni:University ;
                uni:hasName ?universityName .
    FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
  }
  UNION
  {
    ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
               uni:role ?role .
    FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
    FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?professor uni:worksAt ?any }
    ?professor uni:teaches ?course .
    ?course uni:isTaughtIn ?org .
    ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
    ?university a uni:University ;
                uni:hasName ?universityName .
  }
}

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
T4. Appointment-resource bridge (spec axis 2 variant: person linked to orgs
only via an appointment node). Verified on a simulated graph; the rank can
live on the person or on the appointment node — pick what the data shows.

PREFIX uni: <http://university.org/ontology#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?professorName ?universityName WHERE {
  ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
             uni:hasAcademicRank ?rank ;
             uni:holds ?appointment .
  ?rank uni:rankLabel ?role .
  ?appointment uni:atOrganization ?org .
  ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
  ?university a uni:University ;
              uni:hasName ?universityName .
  FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
}

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANTI-PATTERN (do not use). Teaching-only affiliation. Observed on anchor:
joins through uni:teaches are a different data relation; Felix Wagner then
appears with BOTH Institutes (IST and NTUA) and violates exactly-one-row.
Only teaching may be used via the guarded fallback branch of T3.

PREFIX uni: <http://university.org/ontology#>

SELECT ?professorName ?universityName WHERE {
  ?professor uni:hasName ?professorName ;
             uni:role ?role ;
             uni:teaches ?course .
  ?course uni:isTaughtIn ?org .
  ?org uni:partOfUniversity* ?university .
  ?university a uni:University ;
              uni:hasName ?universityName .
  FILTER (regex(?role, "professor", "i"))
}
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Inverse-direction variant sketch (university->department->person): reverse the
triple directions as written in the data, e.g.
  ?university uni:hasDepartment ?org . ?org uni:employs ?professor .
Keep ?university a uni:University ; uni:hasName ?universityName.